1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,000
These are quite large
numbers, so let's take logs.

2
00:00:04,150 --> 00:00:06,550
The log of a product is a sum of logs.

3
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:09,500
We also know how to
take the log of a power.

4
00:00:09,650 --> 00:00:13,650
These integer parts are rather
annoying, so let's drop them.

5
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:15,600
That will give us an error term,

6
00:00:15,750 --> 00:00:18,150
so the equality is no longer exact.

7
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:21,500
Now we can factor out n.

8
00:00:21,650 --> 00:00:23,850
We are left with geometric series.

9
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,200
You surely remember the
geometric series formula.

10
00:00:27,350 --> 00:00:30,950
We got n times the series of ln p/(p−1).

11
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:34,500
Nothing will happen if
we replace p−1 with p.

12
00:00:34,650 --> 00:00:36,650
We're approximating anyway.

